# 7. CHARACTER TASK SYSTEM

## 7.1. Simulated Task Management

Create a frontend-only task system.

Each character should have a task queue containing multiple simulated tasks.

Example tasks:

Research Assistant:
- Collect reference materials.
- Summarize research notes.
- Organize findings.
- Prepare a short report.

Creative Designer:
- Create a moodboard.
- Refine color palettes.
- Review interface layouts.
- Export design assets.

Code Builder:
- Implement a UI component.
- Review component behavior.
- Fix a simulated bug.
- Run a mock quality check.

Documentation Writer:
- Draft documentation.
- Review existing notes.
- Organize project information.
- Finalize a document.

Project Coordinator:
- Review task progress.
- Prepare meeting notes.
- Update the project board.
- Organize upcoming activities.

These tasks are examples. Populate the application with believable, varied task data.

## 7.2. Task Statuses

Support the following task statuses:
- Queued.
- In Progress.
- Completed.
- Paused.

Each task should contain:
- Task title.
- Short description.
- Assigned character.
- Current status.
- Progress percentage.
- Estimated duration.
- Start time.
- Completion time, when applicable.
- Priority.
- Associated room.

Use progress bars or compact visual indicators.

Do not require a backend or external database.

Store initial data locally in the application code.

Use a lightweight client-side state management approach.

## 7.3. Task Progression

Tasks should progress automatically according to their simulated durations.

When a task starts:
- The character moves to an appropriate workstation if necessary.
- Their behavior changes to match the task.
- The task progress indicator updates.
- The inspector reflects the current status.

When a task completes:
- The progress reaches 100%.
- The task status changes to Completed.
- The character performs a small completion animation.
- A completion notification appears if notifications are enabled.
- The activity log records the event.
- The character may begin the next queued task.

Use sensible timing so users can observe the system working without waiting excessively.

For demonstration purposes, simulated tasks may complete in relatively short intervals.

Provide adjustable simulation speed in the settings.

Do not make progress jump randomly without a clear connection to task state.

