# 16. VISUAL POLISH AND MICRO-INTERACTIONS

Pay special attention to small details.

Examples:
- Characters blink occasionally.
- Laptop screens animate subtly.
- Coffee steam rises from mugs.
- Plants sway gently.
- Desk lamps glow.
- Window scenery changes with the time of day.
- Task completion produces a small burst of pixel particles.
- Character selection creates a soft highlight.
- Tooltips appear with a short, smooth transition.
- Panels slide into view without abrupt movement.
- Progress bars animate smoothly.
- Notification cards enter and exit gracefully.
- Room transitions preserve the sense of a continuous world.

Use animation purposefully.

Not every element should move at once.

Create visual hierarchy through differences in movement, scale, color, and timing.

Use restrained easing and short, satisfying transitions.

Support reduced-motion preferences.

Avoid:
- Excessive bounce animations.
- Constant camera movement.
- Distracting blinking effects.
- Unnecessary parallax.
- Random screen shaking.
- Excessive particles.
- Long animations that delay interaction.

The application should feel alive without becoming exhausting to watch.

