# 17. TECHNICAL IMPLEMENTATION

## 17.1. Frontend Only

This is a demonstration project.

There is no backend.

Do not implement:
- User authentication.
- Server APIs.
- Database services.
- External AI model calls.
- Real agent orchestration.
- Real task execution.
- Payment systems.
- Cloud synchronization.
- Server-side notification services.

All characters, tasks, productivity metrics, events, and interactions must be simulated locally.

The application must work as a self-contained frontend experience.

## 17.2. State Management

Create a coherent local simulation model.

The application should maintain state for:
- Characters.
- Character positions.
- Character behavioral states.
- Current rooms.
- Task queues.
- Task progress.
- Task statuses.
- Activity events.
- Notifications.
- Camera position.
- Camera zoom.
- Selected character.
- Selected room.
- Theme.
- Time of day.
- Audio settings.
- User preferences.

Keep the data model organized and easy to modify.

Avoid scattering unrelated hardcoded values throughout the UI.

Use structured data objects for characters, rooms, tasks, and environmental interactions.

## 17.3. Persistence

Use localStorage for appropriate user preferences, including:
- Selected theme.
- Audio preferences.
- Notification preferences.
- Simulation settings.
- Other useful interface preferences.

Use sensible defaults when no saved settings exist.

Handle missing or invalid stored values gracefully.

Do not store large unnecessary animation data in localStorage.

Task and simulation state may reset when the application reloads unless persistence is needed for a coherent demonstration.

Make it clear that the application is a local simulation.

## 17.4. Rendering and Animation

Choose the rendering approach best suited to a richly animated pixel-art environment.

Consider:
- Canvas-based rendering.
- A suitable 2D rendering library.
- DOM-based sprites where appropriate.
- CSS animations for interface elements.

A canvas-based or 2D scene-rendering approach may be preferable for a large world containing many animated characters.

However, use a practical architecture that fits the existing environment.

Keep the application maintainable.

Do not add large dependencies without a clear benefit.

## 17.5. Pixel-Art Rendering

Preserve crisp pixel edges.

For pixel-art images and sprites:
- Use nearest-neighbor scaling where applicable.
- Avoid unwanted image smoothing.
- Keep sprite proportions consistent.
- Use integer scaling when practical.
- Ensure sprites remain sharp at different zoom levels.

Do not stretch sprites disproportionately.

Do not let the environment become blurry when the camera zooms in.

## 17.6. Performance

The simulation should remain responsive during continuous animation.

Implement:
- Efficient animation loops.
- Proper cleanup of timers and event listeners.
- Appropriate frame updates.
- Efficient rendering of characters.
- Limited unnecessary component re-renders.
- Smooth camera transforms.
- Sensible animation throttling when the tab is inactive.

Avoid running a separate uncontrolled animation loop for every character.

Use a coordinated simulation update system where practical.

Test performance with multiple characters moving simultaneously.

## 17.7. No Fake Functionality

Do not create controls that only look interactive.

Every visible interactive control must work.

Examples:
- Theme toggle changes both the UI and environment.
- Camera controls actually move and zoom the world.
- Selecting a character opens the correct inspector.
- Task filters update the visible tasks.
- Notifications can be disabled.
- Music controls operate the actual audio playback.
- Settings change the simulation.
- Room navigation focuses the correct room.
- Task progress updates the relevant character behavior.
- Completed tasks generate corresponding activity events.

If a feature cannot be implemented with the available assets or browser capabilities, provide a working, graceful alternative rather than a nonfunctional placeholder.

